 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS, and the server assigns each committed change an ordered event revision. Self-hosted WebSocket delivery pushes those revisions to connected browsers without introducing a realtime service dependency.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The change store retains replayable revisions and provides a reliable handoff for publication. Delivery owns WebSocket sessions and sends committed revisions. Browsers reconnect after transport failures and catch up from their last applied revision before resuming live updates. This accepts connection-capacity and reconnect operations in exchange for low-latency updates without recurring realtime-vendor fees or provider coupling. Event updates contain stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records.
 
