 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** The server pushes revisioned changes over WebSocket connections and supports replay after reconnect. Five-second HTTPS polling was simpler to operate but added repeated reads and delivery lag; a hosted realtime service reduced connection operations but introduced recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets preserve low-latency delivery and direct infrastructure control at the accepted cost of connection-capacity, reconnect, and catch-up operations.
